# Redeploy the Windows host only when asked

This machine serves `https://bobnpamdrive.com` from the **BnPDrive**
scheduled task (`next start -p 3100`). Code changes are not published until
the user says so. Many edits may land before a publish.

## When to ask

After you finish a batch of app changes (or when the user is about to stop),
ask once whether they want it published. Do not redeploy on every file save
or every small follow-up in the same batch.

Skip the question for docs-only, Cursor-rule-only, or comment-only edits.

If they say yes, run the steps below. If they say no, leave the live site
as-is.

## Steps (only after the user confirms)

```powershell
cd L:\BnPDrive
npx next build
Stop-ScheduledTask -TaskName BnPDrive
Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 3100 -State Listen -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
  ForEach-Object { Stop-Process -Id $_.OwningProcess -Force }
Start-ScheduledTask -TaskName BnPDrive
```

`Stop-ScheduledTask` often leaves the old `next start` process listening on
3100 (the task is already Ready, not Running). Kill that listener or the new
task fails with result 1 and the public site keeps serving the previous
build.

**BnPDriveWatch** (`scripts/ensure-production.cmd`) restarts **BnPDrive**
when `http://127.0.0.1:3100` is not HTTP 200 (boot + logon + every 5 minutes).
Do not disable that task.

Then confirm `http://localhost:3100` returns 200 and the homepage HTML has
the expected new copy (not leftover strings from the previous UI).

If the build fails, fix it and rebuild. Do not restart the task on a failed
build. Do not ask the user to run these commands.

## Do not

- Start `next dev` on port 3100 (it conflicts with the task)
- Leave a Cursor-owned Next.js process running on 3100
- Redeploy without an explicit yes
